# The `make all` Pipeline

`make all` is the headline command. From a clean clone it reconstitutes the whole
stack — backend, data, and frontends — with no manual sequencing. `make all-local`
is a kept alias that runs the identical recipe.

```bash
make all
```

## The stages, in order

The recipe runs these steps front to back. Each one is a no-op when its work is
already done, so re-running `make all` is safe and cheap.

1. **sync-submodules-soft** — best-effort, fast-forward-only submodule sync. Never
   blocks the pipeline; skip it with `SKIP_SYNC=1`.
2. **secrets-ensure** — pulls the shared `*.env` tree from the vault when
   `apps/grobase/.env` is absent and a vault key is present.
3. **certs** — regenerates the local TLS certificates (delegated to grobase).
4. **certs-trust-local** — trusts the local CA in the system and browser stores.
5. **backend-up** — **starts** the grobase backend if it is down.
6. **restore-if-empty** — loads the demo-data snapshot only if the databases are
   confirmed empty.
7. **frontends-up** — builds and starts the frontend services with health validation.
8. **healthcheck → showcase** — verifies the front doors and prints the local URLs.

> [!info] backend-up is no longer just a guard
> Historically it only checked that grobase was running. Now it actually
> **starts** grobase (default edition `migrate`) when the backend network is
> absent, and short-circuits to nothing when the backend is already up. So a clean
> clone plus `make all` brings up *everything*.

## Two ways it can run

| Situation | What happens |
|:----------|:-------------|
| Vault key present | Shared secrets + demo data restored — team parity. |
| No vault key | Fully local: grobase self-generates secrets, env derived locally. |

Both are covered in depth in [The two make all modes](make-all-two-modes.xmd).

> [toggle] The no-vault fallback, in one line
> When there is no root `.env.local`, an `env-local-ensure` step derives it from
> grobase's just-generated `.env` — copying the BaaS keys (they must match
> grobase's random secret) rather than inventing new ones. That is what lets a box
> with only Docker + git + the clone come up with no vault access.

## Related entry points

- `make bootstrap` — a thin alias for this same self-provisioning flow.
- `make -C apps/grobase up EDITION=full` — start the backend by hand for a
  non-default [edition](grobase-editions.xmd).

> [!tip] Re-run freely
> Because every stage is idempotent and `restore-if-empty` never overwrites a
> populated database, running `make all` again after a change is the normal way to
> get back to a known-good stack.
